1. Domain

A domain is a logical environment in which users, computers, and resources can be centrally managed.

A domain commonly uses Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) to provide centralized identity and access management.

Memory
Domain
↓
Logical management environment
2. Active Directory

Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) is Microsoft's directory service used to centrally store and manage information about users, computers, groups, and other resources in a Windows domain environment.

Memory
Domain
   ↓
AD DS
   ↓
Users / Computers / Groups / Resources
3. Organizational Unit (OU)

An Organizational Unit (OU) is a container in Active Directory used to organize objects such as:

Users
Computers
Groups

OUs can also provide a scope for applying Group Policy and delegating administration.

Memory
OU = Organization
4. Security Group

A Security Group is a collection of users, computers, or other groups that can be used to assign permissions and access to resources.

Using groups instead of assigning permissions individually makes administration easier and reduces repetitive configuration.

Example
User
 ↓
Finance Security Group
 ↓
Finance Shared Folder
Memory
Security Group = Access
5. Group Policy Object (GPO)

A GPO is used to centrally apply configuration and security settings to users and computers.

Examples include:

Password policies
Security settings
Desktop configuration
System restrictions
User environment settings
